^["stream"]{
 >> [2026-03-28 14:34] observe — the repo now has a referenced tsconfig graph, but there is no local observatory that can explain whether those changes improved indexing, editor feel, or release surfaces.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:35] observe — current scripts cover build, jsdist, pack dry-run, and several audits, but there is no explicit TypeScript profile/version matrix or projection-friendly reporting path.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:36] observe — the user wants curiosity-friendly testing, not just a one-time optimization: configuration settings, TypeScript 7 comparison, output forms, bundle inspection, and release implication QA should be inspectable and communicable.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:37] decide — this lane should remain local-first and optional: TS7/native preview can be tested, but it should not silently become a default or release gate.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:38] decide — the value surface is not only "faster compile"; it is also explainability. Measurements should be serializable into JSON, Markdown, and .spw so they can feed docs, research notes, and release claims.
 >> [2026-03-28 14:39] taste — the strongest version of this work turns toolchain folklore into reusable engineering taste: what changed, what stayed invariant, and which surfaces were actually affected.
}

^["open"]{
 ?[editor_protocol]: "How much of the editor-indexing profile can be automated locally versus documented as a manual VS Code protocol?"
 ?[preview_install]: "Should TS7/native preview be an opt-in dependency lane inside package.json, a separate script that shells through npx, or a documented external precondition?"
 ?[projection_schema]: "What is the smallest shared schema that can feed JSON, Markdown, and .spw outputs without duplicating interpretation logic?"
 ?[release_gate]: "Which observatory outputs should become release QA signals versus remain research-only?"
}

^["commits"]{
